What you will build
By the end, you will have followed the reasoning behind:

a pinned Conda environment;
a manually validated NGS workflow;
a strict Bash implementation;
a Docker/OCI container image;
Singularity execution on an HPC system;
a five-process Nextflow DSL2 workflow;
local and SLURM execution profiles;
failure tests, caching and output comparisons; and
a versioned, evidence-backed release.

How to use the book
Read the chapters in order the first time. Each practical chapter contains:

a biological objective;
an engineering objective;
a mental model;
small copyable commands;
expected output;
a verification checkpoint;
common failures and their causes; and
a short exercise.

1.11 Nextflow: dependency and task orchestration
Nextflow is a workflow engine. We describe processes with input and output contracts. Channels carry values—such as sample identifiers and file paths—to processes. When all required inputs are available, Nextflow creates a task.
Nextflow can:

stage task inputs into an isolated work directory;
launch ready tasks without waiting unnecessarily for unrelated tasks;
request CPUs, memory and time;
run a task with Conda or inside a container;
submit tasks locally, to SLURM or to other executors;
record logs, traces, timelines and reports; and
reuse a matching successful task with -resume.

Nextflow does not decide whether Bowtie2 is biologically appropriate. It also does not replace Bowtie2: it coordinates the command that runs Bowtie2.
Question answered by Nextflow: Which task is ready, what are its inputs and outputs, what resources/runtime does it require, and can prior work be reused?

1.13 Git and GitHub: traceable change and collaboration
Git records snapshots of selected files and the relationships between those snapshots. It lets a team inspect what changed, why it changed and which exact revision was used. Branches allow work to be reviewed before it becomes part of the main history. Tags can name releases.
GitHub hosts Git repositories and adds collaboration services such as issues, pull requests, reviews, releases and automation.
Git should track source and small evidence artifacts:

Nextflow and Bash source;
environment specifications;
Dockerfiles;
configuration without secrets;
tests and documentation; and
small reports needed to support claims.

Git is generally a poor home for large FASTQ, BAM, index, cache and container files. Sensitive data must never be placed in a public repository.
Question answered by Git: Which source revision and documented decisions produced this project state?

1.20 Try it yourself
Draw the stack for one analysis you already know. It can be an R script, a microscopy workflow or a sequencing analysis. Identify:

input data;
analytical tools;
software environment;
ordering/dependencies;
execution location;
outputs and logs;
version-control evidence; and
acceptance criteria.

If a layer is absent, write “not yet controlled” rather than inventing it. That gap is useful information.

2.2 Engineering objective
This chapter builds a disposable practice workspace and a small evidence file. Nothing touches the bioinformatics pipeline yet. The goals are to:

know where you are;
know what a command will target;
inspect the result;
preserve error information; and
leave a reproducible record.

2.20 Investigate before deleting
This chapter does not require rm. That is deliberate.
Before any future deletion:

print the working directory;
resolve the exact target;
list the target;
confirm that it contains only disposable material; and
prefer a recoverable trash operation when practical.

Never use a recursive deletion command with /, ~, $HOME, an unresolved variable, a broad project root or an uninspected wildcard.

3.14 Hands-on exercise: audit a second execution context
Create a second report named context-comparison.txt.
Choose one comparison available to you:

a new terminal before and after conda activate base;
the workstation versus an HPC login session;
an HPC shell before and after loading a Java module; or
the host shell versus a container shell later in the book.

For both contexts, record:

uname -s and uname -m;
$SHELL and $0;
command -v for Git, Conda, Java and Nextflow;
the first three lines of java -version;
conda info --base when Conda exists;
whether Docker, Singularity/Apptainer and SLURM are expected there; and
one sentence explaining every changed executable path.

Finish with this conclusion template:
Context A is responsible for:
Context B is responsible for:
The most important PATH change was:
The change was expected because:
One missing command that is not an error is:






TipExercise verification hints





Use labeled command groups rather than copying an unstructured terminal history. Redirect Java’s standard error when capturing it:
{
  printf '=== CONTEXT A ===\n'
  command -v java || true
  java -version 2&gt;&1 | head -n 3
} &gt; context-comparison.txt
|| true prevents an expected missing command from stopping a larger strict script, but it also discards that failure status. Use it only when absence is explicitly part of the test.

starting a program is not the same as submitting a job;
requesting 32 CPUs does not make single-threaded software use 32 CPUs;
a job marked COMPLETED may still have produced invalid biological output;
a file can be on a large filesystem while the user’s quota is exhausted;
software can exist on the cluster but remain invisible until a module is loaded; and
activating Conda does not reserve any compute resources.

4.6 Login nodes are coordination nodes
Appropriate login-node work usually includes:

editing scripts and configuration;
inspecting small text files and job logs;
managing Git repositories;
submitting, monitoring and cancelling jobs;
transferring modest files through approved methods; and
performing lightweight environment checks.

Compute-intensive alignment, large compression jobs, broad recursive scans and workflow execution belong in an allocation. A login node is shared by many people; one uncontrolled process can degrade everyone’s session.
The line is set by local policy, not by a universal number of seconds or gigabytes.

4.18 Sanitize before sharing
Inspect:
less hpc-inventory.txt
Remove or replace:

internal hostnames if policy treats them as sensitive;
private project paths;
usernames when unnecessary;
account or allocation names not approved for publication; and
any accidental credential or patient-data reference.

Do not collect env, shell history, SSH configuration or key contents for this artifact.

4.21 Hands-on exercise: review a dangerous AI suggestion
An AI assistant proposes this command on a shared login node:
module load java
conda activate analysis
nextflow run main.nf \
  --reads '/project/patient-data/*.fastq' \
  -with-docker \
  -process.cpus 64 \
  -process.memory 240.GB \
  -resume &
Write ai-review.md with at least twelve findings. Classify each as:

site-policy unknown;
execution-model error;
resource assumption;
software-environment ambiguity;
data/privacy risk; or
verification gap.

Your review must address:

login node versus scheduler allocation;
whether Docker is available or permitted;
module version ambiguity;
module/Conda Java precedence;
unverified partition, account and QOS;
blanket CPU and memory requests;
quoting and wildcard responsibility;
protected-data path disclosure;
detached background execution;
-resume cache assumptions;
missing logs, reports and exit-status capture; and
absence of scientific output validation.

Then write a decision tree, not merely a corrected command:
If this is the login node → submit through the approved scheduler profile.
If the site supports Singularity → use the validated SIF/profile.
If Java comes from a module → verify its path and runtime before Nextflow.
If inputs are protected → follow approved storage and logging policy.
If resources are unknown → profile a small representative test first.






TipExercise reasoning hints





A strong review does not invent the correct partition or account. It identifies missing evidence and states how to obtain it. It also distinguishes operational success from scientific validation.
For every AI-generated line, ask:
What assumption does this line make?
Who owns that decision?
What evidence would falsify the assumption?
What damage follows if it is wrong?
How will success be verified?

a valid access key can still have dangerously broad permissions;
a bucket can contain the right bytes but be in the wrong account or Region;
a successful task can leave large intermediate files accumulating for months;
a container tag can silently point to different software later;
downloading data between Regions can cost money;
a budget notification can arrive after spending has occurred; and
technically correct infrastructure can violate consent, residency or institutional policy.

5.19 Practical acceptance checklist
The paid lab is complete only when you can show:

expected identity and Region were checked before resource creation;
a budget and personal spending ceiling were established;
only synthetic data was uploaded;
public S3 access was blocked;
upload/download bytes matched;
the Batch job reached SUCCEEDED and its log was inspected;
requested CPU, memory and image were recorded;
every lab resource was enumerated after teardown; and
delayed billing was checked later.

5.20 Hands-on exercise: design the next experiment
Design—but do not deploy—a cloud version of this book’s five-process pipeline: raw FastQC, Trim Galore, trimmed FastQC, Bowtie2 alignment/sort and BAM QC.
In cloud-nextflow-design.md, provide:

a diagram showing launcher, S3 prefixes, Batch, compute, registry and logs;
separate human, launcher and task identities;
proposed Region and the evidence needed to approve it;
input, work and result retention rules;
an immutable image reference strategy;
CPU and memory hypotheses for each process;
failure, retry and -resume behavior;
operational and scientific acceptance tests;
a cost model listing every potentially billable component; and
a teardown or production-ownership plan.

Then ask an AI system to review the design. Add a section titled AI claims I verified. For every accepted suggestion, identify the authoritative provider or Nextflow documentation, version and date checked. Reject suggestions that assume permissions, compliance approval or prices without evidence.
